  <h2>How long an AC lasts in Lutz, and when replacement is the better call</h2>
  <p>Plan on 12 to 15 years from a central AC or heat pump in Lutz. National guides say 15 to 20, and that number assumes a system that rests for half the year. Ours runs roughly nine months. What we see in Lutz is a wider spread around that average than in most of our service area, and the reason is the housing stock rather than <a href="/blogs/carrier-vs-trane-vs-rheem-tampa/">the equipment brand</a>.</p>
  <p>The estate systems in <strong>Avila, Cheval and Villarosa</strong> often reach the top of the range or beyond. A variable-speed condenser spends most of the summer loafing at part load instead of slamming on and off, and part-load operation is gentle on a compressor. The 1970s and 1980s homes off <strong>Livingston, Bearss and Lutz Lake Fern</strong> land at the bottom, and it is rarely the condenser's fault. Undersized returns and leaky attic flex push static pressure up, the blower fights it every cycle, and both the blower and the compressor pay for it years early. Replacing the equipment on one of those homes without touching the duct system buys you a newer machine with the same short life expectancy.</p>
  <p>The most expensive repair on any AC system is the compressor, $1,800 to $3,200 here, and on a communicating estate system it is worse, because a failed compressor frequently means a matched coil and a matched air handler rather than a single part. That is exactly the repair where the arithmetic matters most.</p>
  <p>The industry shorthand is the $5,000 rule: multiply the system's age by the estimated repair cost, and if the answer clears $5,000, <a href="/blogs/hvac-replacement-cost-tampa-bay/">replacement usually wins over a five-year horizon</a>. Worked through two real Lutz situations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A 19-year-old single-stage system in a Calusa Trace home needing a $2,400 compressor.</strong> Nineteen times $2,400 is $45,600. Nobody needs a calculator for that one. Replace.</li>
    <li><strong>A 9-year-old variable-speed system in Cheval needing an $1,100 control board.</strong> Nine times $1,100 is $9,900, so the rule says replace, and we would argue against it. The rule is blunt about high-efficiency equipment that has years of service left and parts availability behind it. Repair it, and revisit at year 13.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We run both numbers on the same invoice, tell you which side of the line your system falls on, and say plainly when the rule is giving you the wrong answer for your house.</p>

  <h2>What Florida law actually requires when a rental's AC quits</h2>
  <p>Lutz has a lot of rental and HOA-governed property, and this comes up most weeks in July.</p>
  <p>Florida's Residential Landlord and Tenant Act does not require a landlord to provide air conditioning. <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2024/83.51">Section 83.51, Florida Statutes</a> lists what a landlord must supply in a multi-unit rental, and the mechanical items named are functioning facilities for heat during winter, running water and hot water. Air conditioning is not on that list.</p>
  <p>What does bind a landlord is what the lease promised and what was there at move-in. If the unit came with a working AC system, keeping it working is generally treated as part of the landlord's obligation, and the process for enforcing that runs through <strong>section 83.56</strong>: the tenant delivers written notice describing the problem, and the landlord has 7 days from that written notice to correct it before the tenant's remedies open up. Text messages and voicemails to a property manager are not the notice the statute describes. Put it in writing and keep a copy.</p>
  <p>Practically, for the Lutz landlords and property managers we work with, the fastest path is to get a licensed contractor on the property inside that 7-day window and keep the invoice. For tenants, a documented request plus a dated written notice is what turns "they never called me back" into something enforceable. We are an HVAC contractor and not a law firm, so read the statute yourself at the link above or talk to a Florida attorney before you act on any of it. What we can do is <a href="/services/emergency-ac-repair-tampa/">put a technician on the property quickly</a> and give both sides the same written diagnosis, which is usually what ends the argument.</p>

